# CHANGELOG.md is append-at-the-top and every branch adds an entry, so any two
# concurrent branches conflict there by construction. On 2026-10-31 that
# happened five times in one evening across three agents working in parallel
# (Claude, Cursor, Codex), twice leaving unresolved <<<<<<< markers committed
# to main, and repeatedly re-blocking pull requests that were otherwise green.
#
# `merge=union` takes BOTH sides instead of stopping. That is exactly the
# resolution a human performs every time here — the entries are independent
# prose and keeping both is always correct — so automating it loses no
# information and no judgement.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT COVER. Corrected after the first version of this
# comment claimed more than was ever tested.
#
#   Works:   `git merge` / `git rebase` run on a machine, for a branch that
#            ALREADY CONTAINS this file. union is built into git, so no local
#            config is needed. Verified: #210's own sync with main resolved with
#            no unmerged files.
#   Does NOT: GitHub's own merge. Immediately after this file reached main, a
#            PR whose entry sat at the top beside another merged entry still
#            returned "405 Pull Request has merge conflicts" and reported
#            mergeable_state "dirty". GitHub does not appear to apply merge
#            attributes server-side, so the merge button still conflicts if main
#            gained a CHANGELOG entry since you last synced.
#   Does NOT: a branch cut BEFORE this file existed. git reads attributes from
#            the side being merged into, so the file arrives with the merge and
#            has no effect while it runs. Each such branch conflicts once.
#
# Consequence: sync with main locally before opening a PR and again whenever
# main moves. A PR GitHub reports as conflicted gets NO CI AT ALL — the workflow
# never starts — so a PR with no check runs should be read as a conflict first,
# not as a slow runner.
#
# Deliberately scoped to this one file. Union merge is WRONG for code: it would
# silently concatenate both sides of a conflicting function and produce
# something that compiles and misbehaves. It is right only where the content is
# an unordered list of independent additions.
CHANGELOG.md merge=union
